
## Decision24 amendment: CPU-CI diagnostics preserve process invariants

The corrected independent 5f forensic review (review-and-next-capture.v2.json, SHA256 db3cc89779ec00a7dd5bc31fd7e11ba8673ca776bbd6f5b176714d9a3337ca03) proves that the existing native stack watchdog creates an extra native thread and that two actual tests reject native threads. Exit139 has no retained native backtrace/core and its cause remains unproven. Seven namespace errno13 messages identify neither the failing syscall nor an AppArmor rule. Removing the known observer thread does not establish that imports are thread-free or fix every earlier test failure.

Add only an external CI parent supervising one fresh full-suite child with the existing Python3.12.3, full discovered test selection, required native successes, build/source-before/after/report gates,90-minute job and600000/615000-ms production ABI. Scheduled60s stack requests use a synchronous Python safe-GIL handler; actual request/response times, lateness, missing or coalesced observations replace any promise of timely traces. A bounded parent-drained trace pipe must fail on overflow or observer failure. Preserve the original child's signal/nonzero status and reachable source/lifecycle facts.

A bounded original namespace diagnostic may identify an actual syscall/errno and relevant denial. Only an exact observed userns policy denial for the original interpreter permits a reviewed job-owned ephemeral per-executable profile. Do not change global sysctls, execute a privileged backend, skip strict checks or bump Python. This planning amendment authorizes no CI/source/test edits or workload now; exact review/commit/PR must precede its implementation. Decision23's four-arm component, scientific, physical/cold, final-check and archive/merge requirements remain unchanged.
